b3be4108046" :question/math-academy-id "q-337904"] ["question-887153cd-9896-52b0-b927-cb3be4108046" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\sqrt[3]{-6-3x}=$\n{{field-1}}"] ["question-887153cd-9896-52b0-b927-cb3be4108046" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{-6-3x}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/48/481fd6651401a37e221648026506b508f3ba9984a0637c2f2219c52a5f561b23.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$"] ["question-887153cd-9896-52b0-b927-cb3be4108046" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-c2188212-e382-544c-835c-9192c8bf0f9a" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2188212-e382-544c-835c-9192c8bf0f9a"] ["answer-61606271-882c-55fa-afae-9c47c12185ca" :answer/id #uuid "61606271-882c-55fa-afae-9c47c12185ca"] ["answer-61606271-882c-55fa-afae-9c47c12185ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-61606271-882c-55fa-afae-9c47c12185ca" :answer/value "-\\infty"] ["answer-field-c2188212-e382-544c-835c-9192c8bf0f9a" :answer-field/key "field-1"] ["answer-field-c2188212-e382-544c-835c-9192c8bf0f9a" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] ["answer-field-c2188212-e382-544c-835c-9192c8bf0f9a" :answer-field/correct "answer-61606271-882c-55fa-afae-9c47c12185ca"] ["answer-field-c2188212-e382-544c-835c-9192c8bf0f9a" :answer-field/choices "answer-61606271-882c-55fa-afae-9c47c12185ca"] ["question-887153cd-9896-52b0-b927-cb3be4108046" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-c2188212-e382-544c-835c-9192c8bf0f9a"] [17592186129195 :knowledge-point/questions "question-887153cd-9896-52b0-b927-cb3be4108046"] ["question-fc34fb90-8995-5075-866b-ef20040dee62" :question/id #uuid "fc34fb90-8995-5075-866b-ef20040dee62"] ["question-fc34fb90-8995-5075-866b-ef20040dee62" :question/math-academy-id "q-99792"] ["question-fc34fb90-8995-5075-866b-ef20040dee62" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\sqrt[3]{7-3x}=$"] ["question-fc34fb90-8995-5075-866b-ef20040dee62" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{7-3x}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/5e/5e1d8114757e72cefa7c861cef054545a4adf4868bbb9e602683dc4b960bb428.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$"] ["question-fc34fb90-8995-5075-866b-ef20040dee62" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-4909d287-3318-53e5-9ea2-409a7c8fe123" :answer-field/id #uuid "4909d287-3318-53e5-9ea2-409a7c8fe123"] ["answer-d5db5f04-aa9c-58d3-9a00-7c4085e976a3" :answer/id #uuid "d5db5f04-aa9c-58d3-9a00-7c4085e976a3"] ["answer-d5db5f04-aa9c-58d3-9a00-7c4085e976a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-d5db5f04-aa9c-58d3-9a00-7c4085e976a3" :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}"] ["answer-306bbf6d-7b89-5eb8-ab32-f4ee513fe024" :answer/id #uuid "306bbf6d-7b89-5eb8-ab32-f4ee513fe024"] ["answer-306bbf6d-7b89-5eb8-ab32-f4ee513fe024" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-306bbf6d-7b89-5eb8-ab32-f4ee513fe024" :answer/value "-\\infty"] ["answer-6ec3b1c2-40c7-51fd-ac13-e624c8f2870a" :answer/id #uuid "6ec3b1c2-40c7-51fd-ac13-e624c8f2870a"] ["answer-6ec3b1c2-40c7-51fd-ac13-e624c8f2870a" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6ec3b1c2-40c7-51fd-ac13-e624c8f2870a" :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}"] ["answer-9d4e1826-2dbf-53ab-aee2-bbfdeb3889a5" :answer/id #uuid "9d4e1826-2dbf-53ab-aee2-bbfdeb3889a5"] ["answer-9d4e1826-2dbf-53ab-aee2-bbfdeb3889a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-9d4e1826-2dbf-53ab-aee2-bbfdeb3889a5" :answer/value "\\infty"] ["answer-047ce78e-011e-5d8d-880e-3f85520cc9e0" :answer/id #uuid "047ce78e-011e-5d8d-880e-3f85520cc9e0"] ["answer-047ce78e-011e-5d8d-880e-3f85520cc9e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-047ce78e-011e-5d8d-880e-3f85520cc9e0" :answer/value "\\text{DNE}"] ["answer-field-4909d287-3318-53e5-9ea2-409a7c8fe123" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-4909d287-3318-53e5-9ea2-409a7c8fe123" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-4909d287-3318-53e5-9ea2-409a7c8fe123" :answer-field/correct "answer-9d4e1826-2dbf-53ab-aee2-bbfdeb3889a5"] ["answer-field-4909d287-3318-53e5-9ea2-409a7c8fe123" :answer-field/choices "answer-d5db5f04-aa9c-58d3-9a00-7c4085e976a3"] ["answer-field-4909d287-3318-53e5-9ea2-409a7c8fe123" :answer-field/choices "answer-306bbf6d-7b89-5eb8-ab32-f4ee513fe024"] ["answer-field-4909d287-3318-53e5-9ea2-409a7c8fe123" :answer-field/choices "answer-6ec3b1c2-40c7-51fd-ac13-e624c8f2870a"] ["answer-field-4909d287-3318-53e5-9ea2-409a7c8fe123" :answer-field/choices "answer-9d4e1826-2dbf-53ab-aee2-bbfdeb3889a5"] ["answer-field-4909d287-3318-53e5-9ea2-409a7c8fe123" :answer-field/choices "answer-047ce78e-011e-5d8d-880e-3f85520cc9e0"] ["question-fc34fb90-8995-5075-866b-ef20040dee62" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-4909d287-3318-53e5-9ea2-409a7c8fe123"] [17592186129195 :knowledge-point/questions "question-fc34fb90-8995-5075-866b-ef20040dee62"] [17592186130324 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ below. We will use this graph to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 4} \\sqrt{x} \\, .$\n\n![](images/e6/e6423b96ce60348e780ffd437207bdbeb456e3aae2888d7525f8c4e680f4ee43.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $4,$ the value of $y$ approaches $\\sqrt{4}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} \\sqrt{x} = \\sqrt{4} = 2.\n$$\n\nThe limit of the square root function at a point is equal to the value of the square root function at that point, and this is true for any point *that is greater than $0.$* However, for points that are less than or equal to $0,$ the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,c}\\sqrt{x}=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{c} & \\text{ if }c>0 \\\\ \\text{DNE} & \\text{ if }c\\le 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhy doesn't the limit exist for $c \\leq 0$?\n\n- For $c < 0,$ the limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, c} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist because the square root function $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ is not defined for negative inputs.\n\n- Although $\\sqrt{x}$ is defined at $x=0,$ the left limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0^-} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist because $\\sqrt{x}$ is undefined left of $x=0.$ Consequently, the overall limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist either."] [17592186130325 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our plot of $y=\\sqrt x$ and work out its limits at infinity.\n\n![](images/a0/a04b7cbc735c20a866b1d22287e2f3a1ffc247931c2de08c545cbd2fe922397b.png)\n\nAs $x \\to \\infty,$ the square root function increases without bound. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\sqrt{x} = \\infty.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, when computing the limit as $x \\to -\\infty,$ remember that the square root function isn't defined for $x<0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty}\\sqrt{x} = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186130326 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of the cube root function $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ below.\n\n![](images/20/20deb13b6bce6608c84db84472e19ade1d073c9562f4c414ebee36f4f9e9aebc.png)\n\nUnlike the square root function, the cube root is defined for all real $x.$ As $x$ approaches any finite number $a,$ the limit of the cube root function can be obtained by simply substituting $x=a$ into the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} \\sqrt[3]{x} = \\sqrt[3]{a}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we can see from the graph that the limits at infinity are\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\sqrt[3]{x} = \\infty \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty} \\sqrt[3]{x} = -\\infty.\n$$"] [17592186129334 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0]] "retractions" [[17592186130324 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nbelow. We will use this graph to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 4} \\sqrt{x} \\,$.\n\n![](images/e6/e6423b96ce60348e780ffd437207bdbeb456e3aae2888d7525f8c4e680f4ee43.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $4$, the value of $y$ approaches $\\sqrt{4}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} \\sqrt{x} = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$\n\nThe limit of the square root function at a point is equal to the value of the square root function at that point, and this is true for any point *that is greater than $0$.* However, for points that are less than or equal to $0$, the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\nlim_(x → c)\\sqrt{x} = {\\sqrt{c}\\begin{vmatrix}ifc > 0 \\\\ DNE\\end{vmatrix}ifc \\le 0\n$$\n\nWhy doesn't the limit exist for\n\n$$\nc \\leq 0\n$$\n\n?\n\n- For $c < 0$, the limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, c} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist because the square root function $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ is not defined for negative inputs.\n- Although $\\sqrt{x}$ is defined at $x=0$, the left limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0^-} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist because $\\sqrt{x}$ is undefined left of $x=0$. Consequently, the overall limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist either."] [17592186130325 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our plot of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt x\n$$\n\nand work out its limits at infinity.\n\n![](images/a0/a04b7cbc735c20a866b1d22287e2f3a1ffc247931c2de08c545cbd2fe922397b.png)\n\nAs $x \\to \\infty$, the square root function increases without bound. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\sqrt{x} = \\infty\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, when computing the limit as $x \\to -\\infty$, remember that the square root function isn't defined for $x<0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty}\\sqrt{x} = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186130326 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of the cube root function\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nbelow.\n\n![](images/20/20deb13b6bce6608c84db84472e19ade1d073c9562f4c414ebee36f4f9e9aebc.png)\n\nUnlike the square root function, the cube root is defined for all real $x$. As $x$ approaches any finite number $a$, the limit of the cube root function can be obtained by simply substituting $x=a$ into the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} \\sqrt[3]{x} = \\sqrt[3]{a}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we can see from the graph that the limits at infinity are\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\sqrt[3]{x} = \\infty \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty} \\sqrt[3]{x} = -\\infty\n$$"]]}]